### One More Thing About Commands

Source: https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea/blob/v2.0.0/tutorials/commands/README.md

Commands (Cmds) in Bubble Tea are defined as functions that return a message (Msg). Because they do not take arguments, passing data to a command requires a higher-order function that returns a command. This pattern is particularly useful for performing asynchronous tasks, such as making HTTP requests. It is recommended to perform as much logic as possible within the innermost function, as this is the portion of the code that executes asynchronously, ensuring the main program loop remains responsive.

--------------------------------

### Bubble Tea v2.0.0 Release Notes > Removed Commands

Source: https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea/blob/v2.0.0/UPGRADE_GUIDE_V2.md

A number of commands have been removed in favor of setting fields directly on the `View` struct. For example, `tea.EnterAltScreen` and `tea.ExitAltScreen` are replaced by setting `view.AltScreen = true` or `view.AltScreen = false` respectively. Similarly, mouse motion enablement/disablement commands are now handled by `view.MouseMode`, cursor visibility by `view.Cursor`, bracketed paste mode by `view.DisableBracketedPasteMode`, focus reporting by `view.ReportFocus`, and window title by `view.WindowTitle`.

--------------------------------

### Commands in Bubble Tea > The Initialization Method

Source: https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea/blob/v2.0.0/tutorials/commands/README.md

The initialization method (Init) is responsible for returning the commands that should run when the application starts. It is important to note that you do not execute the command function yourself; instead, you return the function reference to the Bubble Tea runtime. The runtime then manages the execution of these commands at the appropriate time, allowing for a decoupled and managed startup process.

--------------------------------

### Define commands with arguments

Source: https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea/blob/v2.0.0/tutorials/commands/README.md

Demonstrates the pattern for creating Bubble Tea commands that require input arguments. By returning a function that satisfies the tea.Cmd signature, developers can capture scope variables for use in asynchronous operations.

```go
func cmdWithArg(id int) tea.Cmd {
    return func() tea.Msg {
        return someMsg{id: id}
    }
}

func checkSomeUrl(url string) tea.Cmd {
    return func() tea.Msg {
        c := &http.Client{Timeout: 10 * time.Second}
        res, err := c.Get(url)
        if err != nil {
            return errMsg{err}
        }
        return statusMsg(res.StatusCode)
    }
}
```
